# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## Project

{{ONE_LINE_PRODUCT_DESCRIPTION}}. Built with the `physical-product` skill
(patterns2d stack): parametric 2D cutting pattern, marker layout, fabric BOM,
1:1 A4-tiled print sheets.
Pack: robion-maker-pack v{{PACK_VERSION}} — template change log & migration
steps live in MIGRATIONS.md in the pack repo.
This is a product-engineering project, not a software project — no tests or
linters; deliverables are the pattern pieces, the marker, the BOM and the
build sheet. All dimensions are millimetres. Design decisions and the
human-readable BOM live in `README.md`, in the deliverable language.

## Commands

```sh
uv sync                 # once
make pattern            # piece SVGs + marker into out/
make check              # validity gate: outlines, notches, marks, fabric width
make bom                # fabric + notions table into out/bom.md
make tile               # A4 tiling (out/print/pattern_A4.html)
make pdf                # printable 1:1 pattern PDF (needs Chrome)
make previews           # marker + per-piece PNGs — Read before showing
make viz                # hero illustration (out/viz_hero.png)
make buildsheet-pdf     # build sheet PDF (`pdf` is the PATTERN here)
make doctor             # tool availability
```

## Architecture

- `pattern.py` — parameters (single source of truth) → derived values +
  asserts → `PIECES` registry (builder returns the stitch line; the template
  applies seam allowance, unfolds on-fold pieces, packs the marker). Never
  hardcode a dimension inside a builder. Internal markings (stitching
  channels, fold lines, placements) go into `PieceSpec.marks`/`mark_labels`
  — notches are invisible on allowance=0 pieces.
- `tile_a4.py` — windows the packed pieces into overlapping A4 pages with
  registration crosses; page 1 carries the 100 mm control square.
- `viz.py` — flat parametric hero illustration derived from pattern.py.
- Verify visuals by rendering PNG (`make previews`) and Reading every one.
  Never screenshot a mm-sized SVG directly (Chrome ≈ 3.78 px/mm) — always
  go through the pixel `.html` wrappers the scripts write.

## Design record

`design.json` in this folder is the design record: the idea, every answer and
who decided it, the chosen concept, the ten stages with their status, the open
ask and the log of changes. It is written only by `design_record.py` next to it
— never by hand (`python3 design_record.py --help` is the reference). Robion
draws its design panel from it. The rules that keep the panel truthful:

1. Every stop for the maker is one command: `gate <stage> ask.json` (kind
   `questions` | `choices` | `approve`). Never ask in the chat.
2. The maker's reply arrives as one prompt line ("Robion: the reply to …"):
   run `python3 design_record.py show` first, then continue from the stage it
   started. Not replying is waiting.
3. Before producing a stage: `stage <id> working --estimate "…"`; agent-side
   stages close with `stage <id> done --artifact …`; maker gates close through
   the reply.
4. A change after a gate: `change <what> --from … --to … --invalidates …
   --by user|claude` first, then regenerate the stale stages.
5. drawings, bom, plan, analysis may work in parallel (subagents write with
   `--no-commit`); the main thread runs `commit -m "…"` once.
6. `check --strict` before every gate; after a compaction, `show` and `--help`
   are enough to pick the work up.

## Cost of a change

Everything here regenerates in seconds (`make pattern check bom previews
viz`, `make pdf`). The expensive artifact is the user's: a printed and taped
1:1 tiling and cut fabric — so freeze the pieces with the user before
`make pdf`, and say so when a change arrives after printing.

